      ":question/id": "2c4912bd-290a-5074-b11b-459c77b7e238",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "e-451",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\left(\\dfrac{1}{1+2x} + \\dfrac{1}{1-2x}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{1+2x}+\\frac{1}{1-2x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|1+2x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|1-2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|\\frac{1+2x}{1-2x}|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(K|\\frac{1+2x}{1-2x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."
    },
